            %p Here
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the top square is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/naturality+square"} naturality square
                  of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction+counit"} adjunction counit
                  on the right
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitor"} unitor
              %li
                %p
                  the bottom square is that from Prop.
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProjectionComposedWithCounitInRemainingVariable"}
            %p
          %h3#ComparisonOfPushForwardsAndWirthmuelleriso Comparison of push-forwards and Wirthmüller isomorphism
          #PushforwardsIntertwinedByDuality.num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              In a pre-Wirthmüller context, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WirthmullerContext"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast
              are intertwined by dualization, in that there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+equivalence"} natural equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{D}(f_! A) \simeq f_\ast(\mathbb{D} A)
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This is the special case of the property of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi γ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\gamma}
              in prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ComparisonIsEquivalenceOnDualizables"}
              for
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = 1_Y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                      %mover
                        %mi γ
                        %mo ¯
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{D}(f_! A)
                    \=
                    [f_! A, 1_Y]
                    \\underoverset{\simeq}{\overline{\gamma}}{\longrightarrow}
                    f_\ast [A, f^\ast 1_Y]
                    \\simeq
                    f_\ast [A, 1_Y]
                    \=
                    f_\ast \mathbb{D} A
                    \\,.
          #LinearDeMorganDualityForPushForwards.num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              With a Wirthmüller context regarded as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
              (see there), then the statement of cor.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PushforwardsIntertwinedByDuality"}
              is an instance of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+duality"} de Morgan duality
              where linear
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} dualization
              intertwines linear
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi f
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\prod_f \mathbb{D} \simeq \mathbb{D} \sum_f
                    \\,.
            %p
              For more on this see also (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Quantization+via+Linear+homotopy+types"}> Schreiber 14, section 3.3
              ).
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p In a pre-Wirthmüller context
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f_\ast 1_X \simeq \mathbb{D}(f_! 1_X)
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              By cor.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PushforwardsIntertwinedByDuality"}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔻
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi X
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D} 1_X \simeq 1_X
          #AbstractWirthmuellerIso.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Wirthmüller isomorphism)
            %p
              In a Wirthmüller context, def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WirthmullerContext"}
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! 1_X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+object"} dualizable object
              with dual
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+equivalence"} natural equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\omega
                    \\;\colon\;
                    f_\ast f^\ast A \stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow} f_!((f^\ast A) \otimes C)
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#May05"}> May 05, prop. 4.13
            ).
          #MonadCommutesWithDualityUpTotwist.num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              In particular if there is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒴
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \in \mathcal{Y}
              with
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{D}(f_! f^\ast 1_Y) \simeq f_! f^\ast D
            %p
              (hence if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \simeq f^\ast D
              in the notation of prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AbstractWirthmuellerIso"}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PushforwardsIntertwinedByDuality"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f_\ast f^\ast  \mathbb{D}B
                    \\simeq
                    f_\ast \mathbb{D} f^\ast B
                    \\simeq
                    \\mathbb{D}(f_! f^\ast B)
              then prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AbstractWirthmuellerIso"}
              gives a natural equivalence of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔻
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{D}(f_! f^\ast B)
                    \\stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                    f_! f^\ast ((\mathbb{D}B) \otimes D)
                    \\,,
            %p
              saying that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! f^\ast
              commutes with dualization up to a “twist” given by tensoring with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#CartesianWirthmuellerContexts Cartesian Wirthmüller contexts in toposes
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \longrightarrow Y
            any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+geometric+morphism"} etale geometric morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi f
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\sum_f \dashv f^\ast \dashv \prod_f)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathbf{H}_{/X}
                  \\longrightarrow
                  \\mathbf{H}_{/Y}
          %p
            the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+functor"} cartesian closed functor
            (see there for the proof). Therefore any base change of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            constitutes a cartesian Wirthmüller context.
          %p
            Conversely, this means that systems of Wirthmüller contexts are generalizations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrines"}> hyperdoctrines
            ) to non-cartesian contexts (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
          %p
            Notice that in a cartesian Wirthmüller context duality is trivial, in that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔻
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≃
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}X \simeq 1
            for all objects
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            yoga involves duality, it is interesting only the more non-cartesian (non-classical) the ambient Wirthmüller context is.
          %p
            For instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+formula"} projection formula
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi γ
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\gamma}
            in def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ComparisonMaps"}
            for base change along a pointed connected type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G \to \ast
            equivalently says that genuine (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bredon+cohomology"}> Bredon
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            reduces to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+equivariant+cohomology"} Borel equivariant cohomology
            when the action on the coefficients is trivial. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            for more on this.
          %h3#PointedObjects Pointed objects with smash product
          %p
            A first step away from the Cartesian example
            %a{:href => "#CartesianWirthmuellerContexts"} above
            is the following.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathbf{H}
            any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi X
                  %mo ≔
                  %msubsup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{C}_X \coloneqq \mathbf{H}_{/X}^{X/}
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+pointed+objects"} category of pointed objects
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/X}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge_X
            this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}_X, \wedge_X, X \coprod X)
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \longrightarrow Y
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
              restricts to a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast \colon \mathcal{C}_Y \longrightarrow \mathcal{C}_X
              which is a Wirthmüller context.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Shulman07"}> Shulman 07, examples 12.13 and 13.7
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#Shulman12"}> Shulman 12, example 2.33
            ).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \longrightarrow Y
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Y
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast \colon \mathbf{H}_{/Y} \longrightarrow \mathbf{H}_{/X}
              preserves pointedness, and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! \colon \mathbf{H}^{X/} \longrightarrow \mathbf{H}^{/Y}
              preserves co-pointedness. These two functors hence form an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} adjoint pair
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi X
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_1 \dashv f^\ast) \colon \mathcal{C}_X \longrightarrow \mathcal{C}_Y
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              in the under-over category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{/X}^{X/}
              are computed as colimits in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
              with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
              adjoined, and since by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud+axioms"} Giraud axioms
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              preserves these colimits, it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast \colon \mathcal{C}_Y \to \mathcal{C}_X
              preserves colimits. Finally by the discussion at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+pointed+objects"} category of pointed objects
              we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_Y
              are
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
              it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
              has also a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast \colon \mathcal{C}_X \to \mathcal{C}_Y
            %p
              To see that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal functor
              observe that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
              is, by the discussion there, given by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              over
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
              is also a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+closed+functor"} strong closed functor
              observe that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+objects"} pointed objects
              is, by the discussion there, a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
              of cartesian internal homs. These are preserved by
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#CartesianWirthmuellerContexts"} the above case
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
              preserves all limits. Hence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
              preserves also the internal homs of pointed objects.
          %h3#bundles_of_modules Bundles of modules
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Mod
            its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ClMonCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-, R Mod] \;\colon\; Set^{op} \longrightarrow ClMonCat
          %p
            which sends a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            parameterized over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This takes values in Wirthmüller morphisms. (
            %a{:href => "#Shulman12"}> Shulman 12, example 2.2, 2.17
            ).
          %h3#bundles_of_modules_2
            Bundles of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-module+bundles"} (infinity,1)-module bundles
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/master+thesis+Nuiten"} Nuiten 13
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HopkinsLurie"} Hopkins-Lurie
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Homotopy-type+semantics+for+quantization"} Schreiber 14
          %h3#beckergottlieb_transfer Becker-Gottlieb transfer
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfer+context"} transfer context
            is a Wirthmüller context in which also
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast
            satisfies its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+formula"} projection formula
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Becker-Gottlieb+transfer"} Becker-Gottlieb transfer
          %h3#in_equivariant_stable_homotopy_theory In equivariant stable homotopy theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#May05b"} May 05b
          %h3#ForQuasicoherentSheaves
            For quasicoherent sheaves (in
            = succeed "-geometry)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
          %p
            Pull-push of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
            is usually discussed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+context"} Grothendieck context
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
          %p
            Using results of Lurie this follows in the full generality of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+geometry"} E-∞ geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+geometry"}> spectral geometry
            ).
          %p
            Consider quasi-compact and quasi-separated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+algebraic+spaces"} E-∞ algebraic spaces
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+algebraic+spaces"}> spectral algebraic spaces
            ). (This includes precisely those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+Deligne-Mumford+stacks"} spectral Deligne-Mumford stacks
            which have a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scallop+decomposition"} scallop decomposition
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "derived+Deligne-Mumford+stack#RelationToDerivedAlgebraicSpaces"} here
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
            is a map between these which is
          %ol
            %li
              %p locally almost of finite presentation;
            %li
              %p strongly proper;
            %li
              %p
                has finite
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Tor-amplitude
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Tor-amplitude"} ?
          %p
            then the left adjoint to pullback of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
            exists
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo !
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (f_! \dashv f^\ast)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  QCoh(X)
                  \\stackrel{\overset{f_!}{\longrightarrow}}{\underset{f^\ast}{\longleftarrow}}
                  QCoh(Y)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proper+Morphisms%2C+Completions%2C+and+the+Grothendieck+Existence+Theorem"}> LurieProper, proposition 3.3.23
            )
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is
          %ul
            %li quasi-affine
          %p then the right adjoint exists
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (f^\ast \dashv f_\ast)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  QCoh(X)
                  \\stackrel{\overset{f^\ast}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{f_\ast}{\longrightarrow}}
                  QCoh(Y)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quasi-Coherent+Sheaves+and+Tannaka+Duality+Theorems"}> LurieQC, prop. 2.5.12
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proper+Morphisms%2C+Completions%2C+and+the+Grothendieck+Existence+Theorem"} LurieProper, proposition 2.5.12
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+formula"} projection formula
            in the dual form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f_\ast A \otimes B \longrightarrow f_\ast (A\otimes f^\ast B)
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            quasi-compact and quasi-separated appears as (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proper+Morphisms%2C+Completions%2C+and+the+Grothendieck+Existence+Theorem"}> LurieProper, remark 1.3.14
            ).
          %p
            Now if all the conditions on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            hold, so that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi QCoh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi QCoh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_! \dashv f^\ast \dashv f_\ast) \;\colon\; QCoh(X) \longrightarrow QCoh(Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi QCoh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QCoh(X)^{op} \longrightarrow QCoh(Y)^{op}
            exchanges the roles of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast
          %p
            The existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+modules"} dualizing modules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D X = [X,K]
          %p
            is discussed in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Representability+theorems"}> Lurie, Representability theorems, section 4.2
            \.)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier-Grothendieck+context"} Verdier-Grothendieck context
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+context"} Grothendieck context
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+condition"} Beck-Chevalley condition
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambidextrous+adjunction"} ambidextrous adjunction
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The Wirthmüller isomorphism in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
            goes back to and is named after
          %ul
            %li#Wirthmueller74
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Wirthm%C3%BCller"}> Klaus Wirthmüller
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Equivariant homology and duality
          %p see Theorem II.6.2 in
          %ul
            %li#LewisMaySteinberger86
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.+Gaunce+Lewis"}> L. Gaunce Lewis
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equivariant stable homotopy theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/BOOKS/equi.pdf"} pdf
          %p see also
          %ul
            %li#Nardin12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis+Nardin"}> Denis Nardin
              , section 2.6 and A.3 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Stability and distributivity over orbital ∞-categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://hdl.handle.net/1721.1/112895"} hdl.handle.net/1721.1/112895
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.univ-paris13.fr/~nardin/thesis.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A clear discussion of axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            and their consequences, with emphasis on the Wirthmüller isomorphisms, is in
          %ul
            %li#May05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Halvard+Fausk"}> Halvard Fausk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Po+Hu"} Po Hu
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Isomorphisms between left and right adjoints
                %strong 11
                4 (2003) 107-131 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/11/4/11-04abs.html"}> tac:11-04
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0573/FormalFeb16.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Balmer"}> Paul Balmer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivo+Dell%27Ambrogio"} Ivo Dell'Ambrogio
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beren+Sanders"} Beren Sanders
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Grothendieck-Neeman duality and the Wirthmüller isomorphism
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1501.01999"} arXiv:1501.01999
          %p
            Discussion of the original Wirthmüller isomorphism in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li#May05b
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"}> Peter May
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Wirthmüller isomorphism revisited
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/11/5/11-05abs.html"} TAC:11/5/11-05
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0574/WirthRev.pdf"} K-theory:0574
          %p More elaboration of the Wirthmüller context is in
          %ul
            %li
              Baptiste Calmès, Jens Hornbostel, section 4 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Tensor-triangulated categories and dualities
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/22/6/22-06abs.html"} TAC
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0806.0569"} arXiv:0806.0569
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+motives"} pure motives
            includes
          %ul
            %li#Deglise
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+D%C3%A9glise"}> Frédéric Déglise
              , around prop. 1.34 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Finite correspondences and transfers over a regular base
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0765/regular_base.pdf"} pdf
          %p Wirthmüller morphisms between pointed objects and between bundles of modules are discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Shulman07
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Framed bicategories and monoidal fibrations
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1286"} arXiv:0706.1286
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/20/18/20-18abs.html"} TAC
            %li#Shulman12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                %em Enriched indexed categories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1212.3914"}> arXiv:1212.3914
                )
          %p A twisted version of the Wirthmüller isomorphism is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Bastiaan Cnossen,
              %em Twisted ambidexterity in equivariant homotopy theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.00736"}> arXiv:2303.00736
              )
          %p
            Discussion in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+geometry"} E-∞ geometry
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Lurie
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                , section 3.3. of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proper+Morphisms%2C+Completions%2C+and+the+Grothendieck+Existence+Theorem"} Proper Morphisms, Completions, and the Grothendieck Existence Theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                , section 4.2 of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Representability+theorems"} Representability theorems
            %li#HopkinsLurie
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hopkins"}> Michael Hopkins
                ,
